[[:db/cas 17592186622821 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}-4}^{-2}\\frac{30}{|x+4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}-4}^{-2}\\frac{30}{|x+4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186623668 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}\\left(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=5\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$, so replacing $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 5\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 5 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have $h=5,$ and therefore the area of the square at that moment is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{(5)}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{25}{2}=12.5.\n$$" "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}\\left(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=5\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},\n$$ so replacing $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 5\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 5 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have $h=5,$ and therefore the area of the square at that moment is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{(5)}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{25}{2}=12.5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186623693 :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\int }_{e/3}^{e}\\frac{2}{5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x?$" "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{e/3}^{e}\\frac{2}{5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186623850 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\log_{b} a = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare $\\log_{\\frac{1}{5}} \\frac{1}{625} = 4$ with $\\log_{b} a = x$, we get $a = \\frac{1}{625}$, $b = \\frac{1}{5}$, and $x = 4$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to $\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)^{4} = \\frac{1}{625}$. Consequently, $b = \\frac{1}{5}$." "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\log_{b} a = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare $\\log_{\\frac{1}{5}} \\frac{1}{625} = 4$ with $\\log_{b} a = x$, we get $a = \\frac{1}{625}$, $b = \\frac{1}{5}$, and $x = 4$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)^{4} = \\frac{1}{625}.\n$$ Consequently, $b = \\frac{1}{5}$."] [:db/cas 17592186623866 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\log_{b} a = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare $\\log_{\\frac{1}{6}} \\frac{1}{1296} = 4$ with $\\log_{b} a = x$, we get $a = \\frac{1}{1296}$, $b = \\frac{1}{6}$, and $x = 4$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to $\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{4} = \\frac{1}{1296}$. Consequently, $a = \\frac{1}{1296}$." "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\log_{b} a = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare $\\log_{\\frac{1}{6}} \\frac{1}{1296} = 4$ with $\\log_{b} a = x$, we get $a = \\frac{1}{1296}$, $b = \\frac{1}{6}$, and $x = 4$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{4} = \\frac{1}{1296}.\n$$ Consequently, $a = \\frac{1}{1296}$."] [:db/cas 17592186624504 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{8}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{8}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186624516 :question/problem "$\\int (3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186624528 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186624551 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186624555 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186624578 :question/problem "$\\int (5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-3x)\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-3x)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186624579 :question/problem "$\\int (5-8{sin}^{2}⁡2x)\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (5-8{sin}^{2}⁡2x)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186624596 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\pi /20}^{\\pi /20}(2{cos}^{2}⁡(5x)-2{sin}^{2}⁡(5x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /20}^{\\pi /20}(2{cos}^{2}⁡(5x)-2{sin}^{2}⁡(5x))\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186625532 :question/problem "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(-7)=2\\sqrt{5},\\,{f}^{′}(-7)=6\\sqrt{5},\\,g(-7)=\\sqrt{5},\\,{g}^{′}(-7)=-3\\sqrt{5}\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=-7.$" "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(-7)=2\\sqrt{5},\\,{f}^{′}(-7)=6\\sqrt{5},\\,g(-7)=\\sqrt{5},\\,{g}^{′}(-7)=-3\\sqrt{5}\n$$\n\nCompute $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)\n$$ at $x=-7.$"] [:db/cas 17592186625539 :question/problem "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(2)=2,\\,{f}^{′}(2)=1,\\,g(2)=-\\sqrt{2},\\,{g}^{′}(2)=-\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)|}_{x=2}=$\n{{field-1}}" "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(2)=2,\\,{f}^{′}(2)=1,\\,g(2)=-\\sqrt{2},\\,{g}^{′}(2)=-\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nCompute $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)\n$$ at $x=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)|}_{x=2}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186625543 :question/problem "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=-1$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $5$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=-1.$" "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=-1$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $5$ |\n\nCompute $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)\n$$ at $x=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186625552 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{y+x}{xy}\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[1,e]\\times [1,{e}^{2}].$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}\\frac{y+x}{xy}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$  where $R=[1,e]\\times [1,{e}^{2}].$"] [:db/cas 17592186625557 :question/problem "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(11)=-2\\pi ,\\,{f}^{′}(11)=\\pi ,\\,g(11)=4\\pi ,\\,{g}^{′}(11)=-2\\pi\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=11.$" "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(11)=-2\\pi ,\\,{f}^{′}(11)=\\pi ,\\,g(11)=4\\pi ,\\,{g}^{′}(11)=-2\\pi\n$$\n\nCompute $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)\n$$ at $x=11.$"] [:db/cas 17592186625569 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{x+1}$ on the interval $x\\le 1,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}k{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}k{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x &=k{e}^{x+1}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{1}=k{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\nk{e}^{2}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\n$$" "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{x+1}$ on the interval $x\\le 1,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}k{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}k{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x &=k{e}^{x+1}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{1}=k{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\nk{e}^{2}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186625576 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & k\\end{bmatrix}$\nequals $1.$ Find the value of $k.$" "The entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $1.$ Find the value of $k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186625588 :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{R}(-3+2{x}^{3}-3{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,0].$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}(-3+2{x}^{3}-3{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$  where $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,0].$"] [:db/cas 17592186625602 :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ].$ Find $⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ].$ Find $⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).\n$$*"] [:db/cas 17592186625602 :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)}{3}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)}{3}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186625613 :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n${a}_{21}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n${a}_{21}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186625820 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-1,$ $c=1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.\n$$" "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-1,$ $c=1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186626715 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $F$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$ is $\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${f}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{f}_{3}}=\\underbrace{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{f}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(-1)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{f}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-1)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" "First, we reduce the matrix $F$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$ is $\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${f}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{f}_{3}}=\\underbrace{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{f}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(-1)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{f}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-1)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186627673 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int 4{x}^{2}{5}^{{x}^{3}+2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 4{x}^{2}{5}^{{x}^{3}+2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186627685 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int 6{x}^{2}\\,{2}^{2-3{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 6{x}^{2}\\,{2}^{2-3{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186627687 :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then  $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$* $\\,\\times$\n\nBy the invertible matrix theorem, if  $\\text{Col}(A)$ is not ${R}^{n}$ then  $A$ is not an invertible matrix and  so we must have $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then  $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, by the invertible matrix theorem, if  $\\text{Col}(A)$ is not ${R}^{n}$ then  $A$ is not an invertible matrix and  so we must have $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then  $det(A)=0.$* $\\,✓$  \n\nSince $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is not invertible, which means $det(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then  $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$* $\\,\\times$\n\nBy the invertible matrix theorem, if  $\\text{Col}(A)$ is not ${R}^{n}$ then  $A$ is not an invertible matrix and  so we must have $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then  $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, by the invertible matrix theorem, if  $\\text{Col}(A)$ is not ${R}^{n}$ then  $A$ is not an invertible matrix and  so we must have $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then  $det(A)=0.$* $\\,✓$  \n\nSince $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is not invertible, which means $det(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186627689 :question/problem "Write the following series in sigma notation: $\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{25}+\\frac{4}{125}+⋯.$" "Write the following series in sigma notation: $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{25}+\\frac{4}{125}+⋯.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186627692 :question/worked-solution "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix,  then  $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$* $\\,\\times$ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix,  then $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A).$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix,  then $Ax=0$ has only the trivial solution.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\},$ which means that $Ax=0$ has only the trivial solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix,  then  $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$* $\\,\\times$ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix,  then $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A).$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix,  then $Ax=0$ has only the trivial solution.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\},$ which means that $Ax=0$ has only the trivial solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186627714 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}}x{e}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}}x{e}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186628459 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$ Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{csc}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=(-2)^{3}=-8.\n$$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$ Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{csc}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=(-2)^{3}=-8.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186628476 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int x\\sqrt[3]{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\sqrt[3]{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186628482 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{3(x+2)}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{3(x+2)}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186628492 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nLet's first find the value of angle $2\\theta +{30}^{∘}$ where $\\theta ={45}^{∘}$: \n\n$$\n2\\theta +{30}^{∘}=2({45}^{∘})+{30}^{∘}={120}^{∘}\n$$\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${120}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{120}^{∘}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$  \n\nUsing the fact that  $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ and   $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡{120}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{120}^{∘}}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡{120}^{∘} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{120}^{∘}}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta +{30}^{∘})+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2\\theta +{30}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡{120}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sec}⁡{120}^{∘} \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{3})+(-2) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nLet's first find the value of angle $2\\theta +{30}^{∘}$ where $\\theta ={45}^{∘}$: \n\n$$\n2\\theta +{30}^{∘}=2({45}^{∘})+{30}^{∘}={120}^{∘}\n$$\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${120}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{120}^{∘}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$  \n\nUsing the fact that  $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\n$$ and   $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡{120}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{120}^{∘}}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡{120}^{∘} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{120}^{∘}}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta +{30}^{∘})+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2\\theta +{30}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡{120}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sec}⁡{120}^{∘} \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{3})+(-2) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186629273 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$" "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$"] [:db/cas 17592186629275 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{9x-8}{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{41}{16}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$" "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{9x-8}{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{41}{16}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$"] [:db/cas 17592186629286 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186629287 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ using integration by parts. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186629287 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{3}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{5x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{3}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{5}-3\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{5}-3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{5}-3I \\\\ 4I & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{5} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{20}({ln}^{4}⁡x)\\,{|}_{e}^{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({ln}^{4}⁡({e}^{2})-{ln}^{4}⁡(e)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}((2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{4}-(1\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{3}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{5x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{3}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{5}-3\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{5}-3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{5}-3I \\\\ 4I & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{5} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{20}({ln}^{4}⁡x)\\,{|}_{e}^{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({ln}^{4}⁡({e}^{2})-{ln}^{4}⁡(e)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}((2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{4}-(1\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186629296 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186629301 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186629301 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{5}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{5}\\right)]}_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/f2/f2ffb5a9f3439f2ff83ce24e740e762eb34eaa4e52bc9fa244084490e730e159.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{5}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{5}\\right)]}_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/f2/f2ffb5a9f3439f2ff83ce24e740e762eb34eaa4e52bc9fa244084490e730e159.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186629302 :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\n$$  II. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\n$$  III. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186629310 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186629311 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{4x+3}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{4x+3}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186629312 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/2}^{67/6}\\frac{1}{(6x-3)^{1/3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/2}^{67/6}\\frac{1}{(6x-3)^{1/3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186629324 :question/problem "Use the substitution $u={5}^{x}$ to evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{1+{5}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{1+{5}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Use the substitution $u={5}^{x}$ to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{1+{5}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{1+{5}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186629344 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-4x+7}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-4x+7}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186630173 :question/problem "Find ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}(5{n}^{2}+3n+2)$ in terms of $N.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}(5{n}^{2}+3n+2)\n$$ in terms of $N.$"] [:db/cas 17592186630173 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following formulas:\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)$\n\nUsing these results, we can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}(5{n}^{2}+3n+2) & =5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}+3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n+2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}1 \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{N}{6}(N+1)(2N+1)+3\\cdot \\frac{N}{2}(N+1)+2N \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[5(N+1)(2N+1)+9(N+1)+12] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[10{N}^{2}+15N+5+9N+9+12] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[10{N}^{2}+24N+26] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{3}[5{N}^{2}+12N+13]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following formulas:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)\n$$\n\nUsing these results, we can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}(5{n}^{2}+3n+2) & =5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}+3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n+2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}1 \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{N}{6}(N+1)(2N+1)+3\\cdot \\frac{N}{2}(N+1)+2N \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[5(N+1)(2N+1)+9(N+1)+12] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[10{N}^{2}+15N+5+9N+9+12] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[10{N}^{2}+24N+26] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{3}[5{N}^{2}+12N+13]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186630190 :question/problem "Calculate\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}(n-2)(n+2).$" "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}(n-2)(n+2).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186630190 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following formulas:\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)$\n\nWe can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}(n-2)(n+2) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}({n}^{2}-4) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}{n}^{2}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}4 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}{n}^{2}-4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the two series separately. For the first series, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}{n}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 21(21+1)(2\\cdot 21+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 21\\cdot 22\\cdot 43 \\\\  & =3\\,311.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second series, the ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}1$ is just $1$ added together $21$ times, which is $21\\cdot 1=21.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}1=4\\cdot 21=84.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}(n-2)(n+2) & =3\\,311-84 \\\\  & =3\\,227.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following formulas:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)\n$$\n\nWe can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}(n-2)(n+2) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}({n}^{2}-4) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}{n}^{2}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}4 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}{n}^{2}-4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the two series separately. For the first series, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}{n}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 21(21+1)(2\\cdot 21+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 21\\cdot 22\\cdot 43 \\\\  & =3\\,311.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second series, the ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}1$ is just $1$ added together $21$ times, which is $21\\cdot 1=21.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}1=4\\cdot 21=84.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{21}(n-2)(n+2) & =3\\,311-84 \\\\  & =3\\,227.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186630197 :question/problem "Calculate\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{19}9{n}^{2}.$" "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{19}9{n}^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186630241 :question/problem "Find ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}({n}^{2}+n+1)$ in terms of $N.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}({n}^{2}+n+1)\n$$ in terms of $N.$"] [:db/cas 17592186630241 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following formulas:\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)$\n\nUsing these results, we can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}({n}^{2}+n+1) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}1 \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}(N+1)(2N+1)+\\frac{N}{2}(N+1)+N \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[(N+1)(2N+1)+3(N+1)+6] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[2{N}^{2}+N+2N+1+3N+3+6] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[2{N}^{2}+6N+10] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{3}[{N}^{2}+3N+5]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following formulas:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)\n$$\n\nUsing these results, we can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}({n}^{2}+n+1) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}1 \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}(N+1)(2N+1)+\\frac{N}{2}(N+1)+N \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[(N+1)(2N+1)+3(N+1)+6] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[2{N}^{2}+N+2N+1+3N+3+6] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[2{N}^{2}+6N+10] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{3}[{N}^{2}+3N+5]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186630259 :question/problem "Calculate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{36}\\frac{{n}^{2}}{6}.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{36}\\frac{{n}^{2}}{6}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186630907 :question/problem "Express ${[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{4}\\right)]}^{8}$ in the form $x+\\text{i}y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are functions of $\\theta .$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n${[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{4}\\right)]}^{8}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{4}\\right)]}^{8}\n$$ in the form $x+\\text{i}y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are functions of $\\theta .$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\thet