}_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=13-32=-19\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5.4}^{2}}{68}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{82}}≈2.035\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -19\\pm 2.035\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=13-32=-19\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5.4}^{2}}{68}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{82}}≈2.035\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -19\\pm 2.035\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186633595 :question/problem "For the matrix\n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & b & 3 \\\\ c & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},$\nfind the minor ${M}_{32}$ of the element ${d}_{32}.$\n\n${M}_{32}=${{field-1}}" "For the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & b & 3 \\\\ c & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind the minor ${M}_{32}$ of the element ${d}_{32}.$\n\n${M}_{32}=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186633597 :question/problem "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the following matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}?$" "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the following matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186633599 :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes an unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ that's computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nWe're given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{25}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=1500$ and $n=25.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25-1}\\cdot 1500 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot 1500 \\\\  & =62.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $62.5.$" "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes an unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ that's computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nWe're given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{25}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=1500\n$$ and $n=25.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25-1}\\cdot 1500 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot 1500 \\\\  & =62.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $62.5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186633603 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=99-104=-5\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.960:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.960\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{15}^{2}}{20}+\\frac{{12}^{2}}{25}}≈8.084\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -5\\pm 8.084\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $-5\\pm 8.084.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Not all values within the confidence interval are negative:\n\n$$\n(-5-8.084,\\,-5+8.084)=(-13.084,\\,3.084)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ${\\mu }_{x}={\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=99-104=-5\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.960:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.960\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{15}^{2}}{20}+\\frac{{12}^{2}}{25}}≈8.084\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -5\\pm 8.084\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $-5\\pm 8.084.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Not all values within the confidence interval are negative:\n\n$$\n(-5-8.084,\\,-5+8.084)=(-13.084,\\,3.084)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ${\\mu }_{x}={\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186633680 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}(-2)\\cdot (-1)+(-11)\\cdot 1 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ (-11)\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$Hn=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}.$" "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}(-2)\\cdot (-1)+(-11)\\cdot 1 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ (-11)\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle Hn=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186633685 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186633700 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186633701 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}7\\cdot (-4)+(-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-32 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-32 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-4)+4\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-32 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$Za=\\begin{bmatrix}-32 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}7\\cdot (-4)+(-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-32 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-32 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-4)+4\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-32 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle Za=\\begin{bmatrix}-32 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186633704 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&Zn=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}(-11)\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-9) \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-22 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&Zn=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-22 \\\\ 9\\cdot 2+6\\cdot (-9)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-22 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$Zn=\\begin{bmatrix}-22 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}.$" "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&Zn=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}(-11)\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-9) \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-22 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&Zn=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-22 \\\\ 9\\cdot 2+6\\cdot (-9)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-22 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle Zn=\\begin{bmatrix}-22 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186634585 :question/problem "The canonical equation of a line is given by\n$\\frac{x+6}{-11}=\\frac{y-1}{15}=\\frac{z}{-5}.$\n\nFind the vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" "The canonical equation of a line is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x+6}{-11}=\\frac{y-1}{15}=\\frac{z}{-5}.\n$$\n\nFind the vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186634656 :question/problem "The canonical equation of the line is given by \n$\\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{-2}.$\nFind the vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" "The canonical equation of the line is given by \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{-2}.\n$$\nFind the vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186634663 :question/problem "The canonical equation of a line is given by\n$\\frac{x+3}{-4}=\\frac{y+1}{5}=\\frac{z-2}{-3}.$\n\nFind the vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" "The canonical equation of a line is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x+3}{-4}=\\frac{y+1}{5}=\\frac{z-2}{-3}.\n$$\n\nFind the vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186634689 :question/problem "The canonical equation of a line is given by\n$\\frac{x+2}{-4}=\\frac{y+8}{3}=\\frac{z-6}{-9}.$\nFind the vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" "The canonical equation of a line is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x+2}{-4}=\\frac{y+8}{3}=\\frac{z-6}{-9}.\n$$\nFind the vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186634742 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}-0.9 & -0.9 \\\\ -0.4 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}(-0.9)\\cdot (-9)+(-0.9)\\cdot 6 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}2.7 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}-0.9 & -0.9 \\\\ -0.4 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}2.7 \\\\ (-0.4)\\cdot (-9)+0.5\\cdot 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}2.7 \\\\ 6.6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$Hn=\\begin{bmatrix}2.7 \\\\ 6.6\\end{bmatrix}.$" "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}-0.9 & -0.9 \\\\ -0.4 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}(-0.9)\\cdot (-9)+(-0.9)\\cdot 6 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}2.7 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}-0.9 & -0.9 \\\\ -0.4 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}2.7 \\\\ (-0.4)\\cdot (-9)+0.5\\cdot 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}2.7 \\\\ 6.6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle Hn=\\begin{bmatrix}2.7 \\\\ 6.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186634743 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-0.4 & -1.1 \\\\ -0.7 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-0.4 & -1.1 \\\\ -0.7 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186634753 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}-0.4 & -0.8 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}(-0.4)\\cdot 1+(-0.8)\\cdot 1 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-1.2 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}-0.4 & -0.8 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-1.2 \\\\ 0.2\\cdot 1+(-0.6)\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-1.2 \\\\ -0.4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$Xy=\\begin{bmatrix}-1.2 \\\\ -0.4\\end{bmatrix}.$" "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}-0.4 & -0.8 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}(-0.4)\\cdot 1+(-0.8)\\cdot 1 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-1.2 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}-0.4 & -0.8 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-1.2 \\\\ 0.2\\cdot 1+(-0.6)\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-1.2 \\\\ -0.4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle Xy=\\begin{bmatrix}-1.2 \\\\ -0.4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186634767 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & -0.6 \\\\ -0.3 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & -0.6 \\\\ -0.3 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186635478 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the lines $l_1$ and $l_2$ with equations\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\left(\\mathbf{r} - \\langle-1,-2,-1\\rangle\\right)\\times \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle = \\mathbf 0 \\quad$ and $\\quad \\dfrac{x-3}{1}=\\dfrac{y-2}{2}=\\dfrac{z+1}{1}.$" "Find the shortest distance between the lines $l_1$ and $l_2$ with equations\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\left(\\mathbf{r} - \\langle-1,-2,-1\\rangle\\right)\\times \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle = \\mathbf 0 \\quad$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\quad \\dfrac{x-3}{1}=\\dfrac{y-2}{2}=\\dfrac{z+1}{1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186635503 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4{cos}^{2}⁡3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4{cos}^{2}⁡3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186635504 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $r=⟨-5,28,-20⟩+s⟨-2,1,3⟩\\,$ and $\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=5-4t \\\\ y &=8+2t \\\\ z &=12+6t\\end{aligned}\\right.,\\,t\\in R.$" "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $r=⟨-5,28,-20⟩+s⟨-2,1,3⟩\\,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=5-4t \\\\ y &=8+2t \\\\ z &=12+6t\\end{aligned}\\right.,\\,t\\in R.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186635532 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $(r-⟨2,-2,1⟩)\\times ⟨-2,-3,1⟩=0\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{x-3}{4}=\\frac{y-2}{6}=\\frac{z-1}{-2}.$" "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $(r-⟨2,-2,1⟩)\\times ⟨-2,-3,1⟩=0\\,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\frac{x-3}{4}=\\frac{y-2}{6}=\\frac{z-1}{-2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186635557 :question/problem "$\\int ({sin}^{2}⁡8x-{cos}^{2}⁡8x)\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({sin}^{2}⁡8x-{cos}^{2}⁡8x)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186635560 :question/problem "If $x<1,$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{1}\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=${{field-1}}." "If $x<1,$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{1}\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=\n$${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186635565 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the lines $l_1$ and $l_2$ with equations\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 2,-2,16 \\rangle + t \\langle -2,3,-1 \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\dfrac{x-3}{-4}=\\dfrac{y-5}{6}=\\dfrac{z-7}{-2}.$" "Find the shortest distance between the lines $l_1$ and $l_2$ with equations\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 2,-2,16 \\rangle + t \\langle -2,3,-1 \\rangle \\quad$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\quad \\dfrac{x-3}{-4}=\\dfrac{y-5}{6}=\\dfrac{z-7}{-2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186635566 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $(r-⟨-4,1,0⟩)\\times ⟨-1,0,3⟩=0\\,$ and $\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2+2t \\\\ y &=3 \\\\ z &=-6-6t\\end{aligned}\\right.,\\,t\\in R.$" "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $(r-⟨-4,1,0⟩)\\times ⟨-1,0,3⟩=0\\,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2+2t \\\\ y &=3 \\\\ z &=-6-6t\\end{aligned}\\right.,\\,t\\in R.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186635567 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $(r-⟨3,1,2⟩)\\times ⟨-2,4,6⟩=0\\,$ and $\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=7+t \\\\ y &=-4-2t \\\\ z &=2-3t\\end{aligned}\\right.,\\,t\\in R.$" "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $(r-⟨3,1,2⟩)\\times ⟨-2,4,6⟩=0\\,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=7+t \\\\ y &=-4-2t \\\\ z &=2-3t\\end{aligned}\\right.,\\,t\\in R.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186635571 :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\sqrt{t-1})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(t-1)^{3/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(2)=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{2}{3}(2-1)^{3/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{2}{3}+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}(t-1)^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}(t-1)^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ 2(t-1)^{3/2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (t-1)^{3/2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ (t-1)^{3} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ t & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{4}}+1≈1.63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary, approximately, when $t=1.63\\,\\text{s}.$" "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\sqrt{t-1})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(t-1)^{3/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(2)=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{2}{3}(2-1)^{3/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{2}{3}+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $$\n\\displaystyle v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}(t-1)^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}(t-1)^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ 2(t-1)^{3/2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (t-1)^{3/2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ (t-1)^{3} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ t & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{4}}+1≈1.63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary, approximately, when $t=1.63\\,\\text{s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186635577 :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the planes $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_2$ with equations $\\, \\mathbf{r} \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 3 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]} = 6 \\,$ and \n$\\left( \\mathbf{r} -{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}\\right) \\cdot \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}= 0$ respectively." "Find the acute angle between the planes $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_2$ with equations $\\, \\mathbf{r} \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 3 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]} = 6 \\,$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left( \\mathbf{r} -{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}\\right) \\cdot \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}= 0\n$$ respectively."] [:db/cas 17592186636423 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =3+3+1+2 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ C & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})! \\\\  & =3!\\cdot 3!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 2! \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{72}\\,{\\theta }^{9}\\,{e}^{-4\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =3+3+1+2 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ C & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})! \\\\  & =3!\\cdot 3!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 2! \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{72}\\,{\\theta }^{9}\\,{e}^{-4\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636426 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & k & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nequals $\\frac{2}{5}$ and $det(A)=2k+3.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" "The entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & k & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $\\frac{2}{5}$ and $det(A)=2k+3.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186636432 :question/problem "${\\prod }_{p=4}^{k-1}(p-2)=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{p=4}^{k-1}(p-2)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636445 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=20$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=12$ and $n=20.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{12}(1-\\theta )^{20-12} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{12}(1-\\theta )^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=20$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$ \n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=12\n$$ and $n=20.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{12}(1-\\theta )^{20-12} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{12}(1-\\theta )^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636449 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=7$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1+0+0+1+1+1+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=7.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{5}(1-\\theta )^{7-5} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{5}(1-\\theta )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=7$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1+0+0+1+1+1+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=7.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{5}(1-\\theta )^{7-5} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{5}(1-\\theta )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636454 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\prod }_{k=3}^{6}(3k+1).$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{k=3}^{6}(3k+1).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636455 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =2+0+1+5 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ C & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})! \\\\  & =2!\\cdot 0!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 5! \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 120 \\\\  & =240\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{240}\\,{\\theta }^{8}\\,{e}^{-4\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =2+0+1+5 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ C & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})! \\\\  & =2!\\cdot 0!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 5! \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 120 \\\\  & =240\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{240}\\,{\\theta }^{8}\\,{e}^{-4\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636462 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N=3$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{3-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (3-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,3n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{3n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}})$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =3+2+2+1+1+1 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{3(6)-10} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N=3$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{3-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (3-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,3n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{3n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}})\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =3+2+2+1+1+1 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{3(6)-10} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636463 :question/problem "${\\prod }_{n=2}^{p}\\frac{{n}^{2}}{(n+1)(n-1)}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{n=2}^{p}\\frac{{n}^{2}}{(n+1)(n-1)}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636471 :question/problem "Consider the matrix\n    $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhat are the dimensions of ${B}^{T}?$" "Consider the matrix\n    $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhat are the dimensions of ${B}^{T}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186636473 :question/problem "${\\prod }_{i=2}^{5}(3i-2)=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{i=2}^{5}(3i-2)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636474 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =3+2+1+3+2+1 \\\\  & =12 \\\\ C & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})! \\\\  & =3!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 3!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 1! \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\cdot 6\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{144}\\,{\\theta }^{12}\\,{e}^{-6\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =3+2+1+3+2+1 \\\\  & =12 \\\\ C & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})! \\\\  & =3!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 3!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 1! \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\cdot 6\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{144}\\,{\\theta }^{12}\\,{e}^{-6\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636476 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=9$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=6$ and $n=9.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{6}(1-\\theta )^{9-6} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{6}(1-\\theta )^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=9$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$ \n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=6\n$$ and $n=9.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{6}(1-\\theta )^{9-6} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{6}(1-\\theta )^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636477 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+1.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+\\frac{1}{2})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+\\frac{1}{2})$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{-\\frac{1}{2}} & 4 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\  &  & -2 & 2 & -1 \\\\  & 4 & -4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{2})(4{x}^{2}-4x+2).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $4{x}^{2}-4x+2=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-4x+2 & =0 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\text{i} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$" "Let $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+1.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+\\frac{1}{2})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+\\frac{1}{2})$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{-\\frac{1}{2}} & 4 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\  &  & -2 & 2 & -1 \\\\  & 4 & -4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{2})(4{x}^{2}-4x+2).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $4{x}^{2}-4x+2=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-4x+2 & =0 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\text{i} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $$\n\\displaystyle x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636484 :question/problem "$\\frac{5}{5+8}\\cdot \\frac{6}{6+8}\\cdot \\frac{7}{7+8}\\cdot \\,…\\,\\cdot \\frac{20}{20+8}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{5+8}\\cdot \\frac{6}{6+8}\\cdot \\frac{7}{7+8}\\cdot \\,…\\,\\cdot \\frac{20}{20+8}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636485 :question/problem "${\\prod }_{j=2}^{p}\\frac{(j+1)(j-1)}{j}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{j=2}^{p}\\frac{(j+1)(j-1)}{j}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636487 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=15$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N=2$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{2-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (2-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,2n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{2n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=10$ and $n=15.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{2(15)-10} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=15$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N=2$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{2-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (2-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,2n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{2n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=10\n$$ and $n=15.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{2(15)-10} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636493 :question/problem "${\\prod }_{i=1}^{3}\\frac{6i}{5i-1}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{i=1}^{3}\\frac{6i}{5i-1}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636495 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N=5$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{5-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,3,4,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (5-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,5n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{5n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =4+3+1+2+1 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{11}(1-\\theta )^{5(5)-11} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{11}(1-\\theta )^{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N=5$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{5-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,3,4,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (5-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,5n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{5n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =4+3+1+2+1 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{11}(1-\\theta )^{5(5)-11} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{11}(1-\\theta )^{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636502 :question/problem "Find ${S}^{T}$ given that \n    $S=\\begin{bmatrix}a & -b & c\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find ${S}^{T}$ given that \n    $$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}a & -b & c\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636509 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\prod }_{i=3}^{6}\\frac{3i-1}{3i+1}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{i=3}^{6}\\frac{3i-1}{3i+1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636512 :question/problem "${\\prod }_{p=3}^{k}({p}^{2}-4)=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{p=3}^{k}({p}^{2}-4)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636514 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=9$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=12,$ $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!=16,$ and $n=9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{16}\\,{\\theta }^{12}\\,{e}^{-9\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=9$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=12,\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!=16,\n$$ and $n=9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{16}\\,{\\theta }^{12}\\,{e}^{-9\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636523 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=11$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=14,$ $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!=48,$ and $n=11.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{48}\\,{\\theta }^{14}\\,{e}^{-11\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=11$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=14,\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!=48,\n$$ and $n=11.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{48}\\,{\\theta }^{14}\\,{e}^{-11\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636524 :question/problem "${\\prod }_{j=2}^{5}(j+2)=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{j=2}^{5}(j+2)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636528 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1+0+1+0+0+0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{2}(1-\\theta )^{6-2} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{2}(1-\\theta )^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1+0+1+0+0+0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{2}(1-\\theta )^{6-2} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{2}(1-\\theta )^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636538 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=7$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N=2$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{2-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (2-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,2n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{2n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1+1+2+1+1+2+1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{9}(1-\\theta )^{2(7)-9} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{9}(1-\\theta )^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=7$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N=2$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{2-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (2-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,2n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{2n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1+1+2+1+1+2+1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{9}(1-\\theta )^{2(7)-9} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{9}(1-\\theta )^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637237 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}{2}^{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}{2}^{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186637238 :question/problem "Given that the determinant of \n$\\begin{bmatrix}1-2p & 4 & 2p+1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ \nis $-126,$  find the value of $p.$" "Given that the determinant of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1-2p & 4 & 2p+1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nis $-126,$  find the value of $p.$"] [:db/cas 17592186637242 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=30$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $16$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =16\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(30-16)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +14\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=30$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $16$ 