c{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3\\cdot 1\\cdot 3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =27,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=1+2+3+1+3=10.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(27)+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(3\\cdot 5-10)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡27+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=27+10+5=42.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637259 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 4\\,{e}^{3-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 4\\,{e}^{3-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637266 :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}(-{sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}(-{sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637268 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=12$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +1){x}^{-\\theta -2},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}((\\theta +1){x}_{i}^{-\\theta -2}) \\\\  & =(\\theta +1)^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -2} \\\\  & =(\\theta +1)^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=20.5$ and $n=12.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=(\\theta +1)^{12}(20.5)^{-\\theta -2}.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=12$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +1){x}^{-\\theta -2},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}((\\theta +1){x}_{i}^{-\\theta -2}) \\\\  & =(\\theta +1)^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -2} \\\\  & =(\\theta +1)^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=20.5\n$$ and $n=12.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=(\\theta +1)^{12}(20.5)^{-\\theta -2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637270 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}4{e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}4{e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186637281 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=7$ and $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{6}\\,{e}^{-7\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$ \n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=7\n$$ and $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{6}\\,{e}^{-7\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637284 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1.4+0.8+2.5+3.1 \\\\  & =7.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{4}\\,{e}^{-7.8\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1.4+0.8+2.5+3.1 \\\\  & =7.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{4}\\,{e}^{-7.8\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637288 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N=2$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{2-x},\\,x=0,1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(2-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(2-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(2n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{3}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{4}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{5}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{6}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(2n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=1+2+2+1+2+2=10.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(2\\cdot 6-10)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+10+2=16.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N=2$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{2-x},\\,x=0,1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(2-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(2-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(2n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{3}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{4}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{5}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{6}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(2n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=1+2+2+1+2+2=10.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(2\\cdot 6-10)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+10+2=16.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637290 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=17$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +4){x}^{-\\theta -5},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}((\\theta +4){x}_{i}^{-\\theta -5}) \\\\  & =(\\theta +4)^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -5} \\\\  & =(\\theta +4)^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=24.3$ and $n=17.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=(\\theta +4)^{17}(24.3)^{-\\theta -5}.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=17$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +4){x}^{-\\theta -5},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}((\\theta +4){x}_{i}^{-\\theta -5}) \\\\  & =(\\theta +4)^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -5} \\\\  & =(\\theta +4)^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=24.3\n$$ and $n=17.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=(\\theta +4)^{17}(24.3)^{-\\theta -5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637293 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-2}^{0}{7}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{0}{7}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{7}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{7}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186637297 :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{1}^{2}{6}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}{6}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637321 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=18$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $10$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(18-10)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=18$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $10$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(18-10)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637326 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +2){x}^{-\\theta -3},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}((\\theta +2){x}_{i}^{-\\theta -3}) \\\\  & =(\\theta +2)^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -3} \\\\  & =(\\theta +2)^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=14.7$ and $n=10.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=(\\theta +2)^{10}(14.7)^{-\\theta -3}.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +2){x}^{-\\theta -3},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}((\\theta +2){x}_{i}^{-\\theta -3}) \\\\  & =(\\theta +2)^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -3} \\\\  & =(\\theta +2)^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=14.7\n$$ and $n=10.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=(\\theta +2)^{10}(14.7)^{-\\theta -3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637554 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}\\frac{9}{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}\\frac{9}{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637666 :question/problem "Calculate $\\text{det}(M)$, if\n$M=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate $\\text{det}(M)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637671 :question/problem "Evaluate the determinant:\n\n$\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186637682 :question/problem "Calculate $\\text{det}(M)$, if\n$M=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate $\\text{det}(M)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637686 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637691 :question/problem "Calculate $\\text{det}(X)$, if\n$X=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate $\\text{det}(X)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186638262 :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{bmatrix},$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,1).$" "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186638262 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{bmatrix},$\nwe must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{2}-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}\\,-\\,1)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)=2y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}\\,-\\,1)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=0-2\\cdot 2=-4.\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{2}-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}\\,-\\,1)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)=2y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}\\,-\\,1)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=0-2\\cdot 2=-4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186638266 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(1,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{2}-y & 2y{x}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix}$" "Find ${f}^{″}(1,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{2}-y & 2y{x}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186638272 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x-3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x-3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186638274 :question/problem "Calculate ${C}^{-1}$, where\n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${C}^{-1}=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | $1$ |  |" "Calculate ${C}^{-1}$, where\n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${C}^{-1}=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | $1$ |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186638276 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}-y+1 & -x\\end{bmatrix}$" "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}-y+1 & -x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639188 :question/problem "Given that\n$S=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{23}=0?$" "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{23}=0?$"] [:db/cas 17592186639199 :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that  ${x}_{3}=5.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-5=-\\frac{3}{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}+5=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=5$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{7}{2}$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)-(5)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}+5+7=\\frac{25}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{25}{2}$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{7}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=5.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" "The third equation states that  ${x}_{3}=5.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-5=-\\frac{3}{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}+5=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=5$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{7}{2}$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)-(5)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}+5+7=\\frac{25}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{25}{2}$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{7}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=5.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$$\n\\displaystyle =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639230 :question/problem "${\\left(-\\frac{k}{l}\\right)}^{3}{\\left(-\\frac{m}{n}\\right)}^{4}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(-\\frac{k}{l}\\right)}^{3}{\\left(-\\frac{m}{n}\\right)}^{4}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639236 :question/problem "Find the value of $c$ such that $\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right)}^{2}=c{x}^{2}.$" "Find the value of $c$ such that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right)}^{2}=c{x}^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639242 :question/problem "${\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{6}{\\left(\\frac{4}{x}\\right)}^{2}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{6}{\\left(\\frac{4}{x}\\right)}^{2}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639264 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y+3z &=-2 \\\\ y+z &=2 \\\\ -z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n\n$$\n-z=3\\,⟹\\,z=-3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(-3)=2\\,⟹\\,y=2+3=5.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-3$ and $y=5$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x+3(5)+3(-3)=-2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{-2-15+9}{2}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-4$, $y=5$, and $z=-3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as \n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y+3z &=-2 \\\\ y+z &=2 \\\\ -z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n\n$$\n-z=3\\,⟹\\,z=-3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(-3)=2\\,⟹\\,y=2+3=5.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-3$ and $y=5$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x+3(5)+3(-3)=-2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{-2-15+9}{2}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-4$, $y=5$, and $z=-3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639317 :question/problem "If\n    $U=\\begin{bmatrix}-3v & -7 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-16$, what is the value of $v?$" "If\n    $$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}-3v & -7 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-16$, what is the value of $v?$"] [:db/cas 17592186639318 :question/problem "If\n$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-6-8r & 2-6r \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(U)=182$, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}-6-8r & 2-6r \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(U)=182$, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186639325 :question/problem "If\n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\text{det}(C)=16,$ determine the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(C)=16,$ determine the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186639327 :question/problem "If\n    $U=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ -4k & 8k+2\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-6$, what is the value of $k?$" "If\n    $$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ -4k & 8k+2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-6$, what is the value of $k?$"] [:d