(2x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186640046 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186640046 :question/worked-solution "We use the identities $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ and $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}-2) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identities $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}-2) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186640764 :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x={t}^{3}+2,\\,y={t}^{4}+5.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4}{3}t,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,3}=$  \n{{field-1}}" "A curve has parametric equations $x={t}^{3}+2,\\,y={t}^{4}+5.$ Given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4}{3}t,\n$$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,3}=$  \n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186641048 :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- \\frac{e^{- \\frac{x}{4}}}{4}$?" "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- \\frac{e^{- \\frac{x}{4}}}{4}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186641052 :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{e^{- \\frac{x}{4}}}{4}$?" "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{e^{- \\frac{x}{4}}}{4}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186641053 :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{e^{\\frac{x}{2}}}{4}$?" "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{e^{\\frac{x}{2}}}{4}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186641596 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641617 :question/problem "Calculate\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}5{n}^{3}.$" "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}5{n}^{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641625 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-1,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3+5t \\\\ y &=4t \\\\ z &=2-t\\end{aligned}\\right..$" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-1,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3+5t \\\\ y &=4t \\\\ z &=2-t\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641627 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186641638 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4},$ find  $∇f$ using the definition." "Given the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4},\n$$ find  $∇f$ using the definition."] [:db/cas 17592186641641 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-{csc}^{2}⁡x}{{csc}^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}-{cot}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-{csc}^{2}⁡x}{{csc}^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}-{cot}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186641644 :question/problem "Find ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}(2{n}^{3}+3{n}^{2}+2n+4)$ in terms of $N.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}(2{n}^{3}+3{n}^{2}+2n+4)\n$$ in terms of $N.$"] [:db/cas 17592186641644 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formulas:\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{N}^{2}(N+1)^{2}=({\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n)^{2}$\n\nWe can evaluate the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}(2{n}^{3}+3{n}^{2}+2n+4) & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{3}+3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}+2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{N}^{2}}{4}(N+1)^{2}+3\\cdot \\frac{N}{6}(N+1)(2N+1)+2\\cdot \\frac{N}{2}(N+1)+4N \\\\  & =\\frac{{N}^{2}}{2}(N+1)^{2}+\\frac{N}{2}(N+1)(2N+1)+N(N+1)+4N \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{2}[N(N+1)^{2}+(N+1)(2N+1)+2(N+1)+8] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{2}[N({N}^{2}+2N+1)+(2{N}^{2}+3N+1)+2(N+1)+8] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{2}[{N}^{3}+2{N}^{2}+N+2{N}^{2}+3N+1+2N+2+8] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{2}[{N}^{3}+4{N}^{2}+6N+11]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we recall the following formulas:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{N}^{2}(N+1)^{2}=({\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}(2{n}^{3}+3{n}^{2}+2n+4) & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{3}+3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}+2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{N}^{2}}{4}(N+1)^{2}+3\\cdot \\frac{N}{6}(N+1)(2N+1)+2\\cdot \\frac{N}{2}(N+1)+4N \\\\  & =\\frac{{N}^{2}}{2}(N+1)^{2}+\\frac{N}{2}(N+1)(2N+1)+N(N+1)+4N \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{2}[N(N+1)^{2}+(N+1)(2N+1)+2(N+1)+8] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{2}[N({N}^{2}+2N+1)+(2{N}^{2}+3N+1)+2(N+1)+8] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{2}[{N}^{3}+2{N}^{2}+N+2{N}^{2}+3N+1+2N+2+8] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{2}[{N}^{3}+4{N}^{2}+6N+11]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641652 :question/problem "Calculate\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(4{n}^{3}-3{n}^{2}).$" "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(4{n}^{3}-3{n}^{2}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641652 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formulas:\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{N}^{2}(N+1)^{2}=({\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n)^{2}$\n\nWe can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(4{n}^{3}-3{n}^{2}) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}4{n}^{3}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}3{n}^{2} \\\\  & =4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}{n}^{3}-3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}{n}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the two series separately.  For the first series we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}{n}^{3} & =4(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {22}^{2}(22+1)^{2}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {22}^{2}\\cdot {23}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\cancel{4}(\\frac{1}{\\cancel{4}}\\cdot {22}^{2}\\cdot {23}^{2}) \\\\  & =256\\,036.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second series, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}{n}^{2} & =3(\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 22(22+1)(2\\cdot 22+1)) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 22\\cdot 23\\cdot 45) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3\\,795 \\\\  & =11\\,385.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(4{n}^{3}-3{n}^{2}) & =256\\,036-11\\,385 \\\\  & =244\\,651.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we recall the following formulas:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{N}^{2}(N+1)^{2}=({\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(4{n}^{3}-3{n}^{2}) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}4{n}^{3}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}3{n}^{2} \\\\  & =4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}{n}^{3}-3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}{n}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the two series separately.  For the first series we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}{n}^{3} & =4(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {22}^{2}(22+1)^{2}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {22}^{2}\\cdot {23}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\cancel{4}(\\frac{1}{\\cancel{4}}\\cdot {22}^{2}\\cdot {23}^{2}) \\\\  & =256\\,036.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second series, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}{n}^{2} & =3(\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 22(22+1)(2\\cdot 22+1)) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 22\\cdot 23\\cdot 45) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3\\,795 \\\\  & =11\\,385.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(4{n}^{3}-3{n}^{2}) & =256\\,036-11\\,385 \\\\  & =244\\,651.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641659 :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{11}(-3{n}^{3})=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{11}(-3{n}^{3})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641752 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{5} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta+6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos^{4} \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{3} \\theta}$." "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{6\\,\\sin^{5} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta+6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos^{4} \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{3} \\theta}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641765 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{4} \\theta+\\sin^{4} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta}$." "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{4} \\theta+\\sin^{4} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641767 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{2\\,\\sin^{3} t\\,\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{4} t\\,\\cos^{2} t+\\sin^{2} t\\,\\cos^{4} t}$." "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\,\\sin^{3} t\\,\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{4} t\\,\\cos^{2} t+\\sin^{2} t\\,\\cos^{4} t}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641777 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{3\\,\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{3} \\phi}{\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{4} \\phi+\\sin^{3} \\phi\\,\\cos^{2} \\phi}$." "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3\\,\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{3} \\phi}{\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{4} \\phi+\\sin^{3} \\phi\\,\\cos^{2} \\phi}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641778 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos^{3} \\theta+6\\,\\sin^{5} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}$." "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos^{3} \\theta+6\\,\\sin^{5} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186642299 :question/problem "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)=1$\n\nII. ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist\n\nIII. $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$" "Let \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)=1$\n\nII. ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist\n\nIII. $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$"] [:db/cas 17592186642303 :question/problem "The canonical equation of a line is given by\n$\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}.$\nFind a vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" "The canonical equation of a line is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}.\n$$\nFind a vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186642349 :question/problem "The canonical equation of a line is given by\n$\\frac{x-6}{5}=\\frac{y-7}{4}=\\frac{z+3}{7}.$\n\nFind the vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" "The canonical equation of a line is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x-6}{5}=\\frac{y-7}{4}=\\frac{z+3}{7}.\n$$\n\nFind the vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186642443 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\cos \\alpha(\\frac{4\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}+\\frac{3\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}).$" "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\cos \\alpha(\\frac{4\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}+\\frac{3\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186642470 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and $B$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $A$ and $3$ rows in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-1=1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 11 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,11=1\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 11 \\\\ -8 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-8=(-2)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 11 \\\\ -8 & -14\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-14=(-2)\\cdot 3+4\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$AB=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 11 \\\\ -8 & -14\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and $B$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $A$ and $3$ rows in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-1=1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 11 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,11=1\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 11 \\\\ -8 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-8=(-2)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 11 \\\\ -8 & -14\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-14=(-2)\\cdot 3+4\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle AB=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 11 \\\\ -8 & -14\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186642471 :question/worked-solution "Here, $E$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix and $F$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $4$ columns in $E$ and $4$ rows in $F,$ so $E$ and $F$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{E}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{F}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}={EF}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $EF,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-10=(-1)\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 0+1\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -4 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-4=(-1)\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -4 \\\\ -3 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-3=(-2)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 0+0\\cdot (-4)+(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-2=(-2)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$EF=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Here, $E$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix and $F$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $4$ columns in $E$ and $4$ rows in $F,$ so $E$ and $F$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{E}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{F}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}={EF}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $EF,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-10=(-1)\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 0+1\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -4 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-4=(-1)\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -4 \\\\ -3 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-3=(-2)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 0+0\\cdot (-4)+(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-2=(-2)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle EF=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186642479 :question/worked-solution "Here, $X$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix and $Y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $X$ and $3$ rows in $Y,$ so $X$ and $Y$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{X}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{Y}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={XY}_{1\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $XY,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,8=(-2)\\cdot (-4)+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,10=(-2)\\cdot (-5)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$XY=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Here, $X$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix and $Y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $X$ and $3$ rows in $Y,$ so $X$ and $Y$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{X}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{Y}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={XY}_{1\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $XY,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,8=(-2)\\cdot (-4)+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,10=(-2)\\cdot (-5)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle XY=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186642482 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and $B$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $A$ and $3$ rows in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,4=4\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 5+(-4)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,8=4\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ -10 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-10=1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 5+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ -10 & -5\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-5=1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$AB=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ -10 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and $B$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $A$ and $3$ rows in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,4=4\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 5+(-4)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,8=4\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ -10 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-10=1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 5+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ -10 & -5\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,-5=1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle AB=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ -10 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186642486 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix and $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere are $2$ columns in $A$ and $2$ rows in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 3}={AB}_{1\\times 3}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,5=1\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,5=1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,1=1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$AB=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Here, $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix and $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere are $2$ columns in $A$ and $2$ rows in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 3}={AB}_{1\\times 3}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,5=1\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,5=1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ & \\,1=1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle AB=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186642503 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $-2\\cos \\theta\\sin \\theta\\left(\\frac{\\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta}+\\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}\\right).$ Assume $\\sin \\theta\\ne0$ and $\\cos \\theta\\ne0$." "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle -2\\cos \\theta\\sin \\theta\\left(\\frac{\\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta}+\\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}\\right).\n$$ Assume $\\sin \\theta\\ne0$ and $\\cos \\theta\\ne0$."] [:db/cas 17592186642580 :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the second term using $\\tan \\alpha=\\frac{\\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{1+2\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}+4\\cdot \\tan \\alpha \\\\ &= \\frac{1+2\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}+\\frac{4\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPut both terms over a common denominator and combine the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{(1+2\\cdot \\cos \\alpha)\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}+\\frac{4\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\cos \\alpha+2\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\alpha+4\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGroup the squared terms and apply the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{\\cos \\alpha+4\\cdot (\\cos^{2} \\alpha+\\sin^{2} \\alpha)-2\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\cos \\alpha+4-2\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere we used $\\sin^{2} \\alpha+\\cos^{2} \\alpha=1$. Therefore, the simplified expression is $\\frac{\\cos \\alpha+4-2\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}$, on the original domain $\\sin \\alpha\\ne0, \\cos \\alpha\\ne0$." "First, rewrite the second term using $\\tan \\alpha=\\frac{\\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{1+2\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}+4\\cdot \\tan \\alpha \\\\ &= \\frac{1+2\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}+\\frac{4\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPut both terms over a common denominator and combine the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{(1+2\\cdot \\cos \\alpha)\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}+\\frac{4\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\cos \\alpha+2\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\alpha+4\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGroup the squared terms and apply the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{\\cos \\alpha+4\\cdot (\\cos^{2} \\alpha+\\sin^{2} \\alpha)-2\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\cos \\alpha+4-2\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere we used $\\sin^{2} \\alpha+\\cos^{2} \\alpha=1$. Therefore, the simplified expression is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos \\alpha+4-2\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha},\n$$ on the original domain $\\sin \\alpha\\ne0, \\cos \\alpha\\ne0$."] [:db/cas 17592186642585 :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the second term using $\\cot \\alpha=\\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{2+3\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}+\\cot \\alpha \\\\ &= \\frac{2+3\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}+\\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPut both terms over a common denominator and combine the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{(2+3\\cdot \\sin \\alpha)\\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}+\\frac{\\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot \\sin \\alpha+3\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha+\\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGroup the squared terms and apply the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{2\\cdot \\sin \\alpha+(\\sin^{2} \\alpha+\\cos^{2} \\alpha)+2\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot \\sin \\alpha+1+2\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere we used $\\cos^{2} \\alpha+\\sin^{2} \\alpha=1$. Therefore, the simplified expression is $\\frac{2\\cdot \\sin \\alpha+1+2\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}$, on the original domain $\\cos \\alpha\\ne0, \\sin \\alpha\\ne0$." "First, rewrite the second term using $\\cot \\alpha=\\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{2+3\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}+\\cot \\alpha \\\\ &= \\frac{2+3\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}+\\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPut both terms over a common denominator and combine the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{(2+3\\cdot \\sin \\alpha)\\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}+\\frac{\\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot \\sin \\alpha+3\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha+\\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGroup the squared terms and apply the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{2\\cdot \\sin \\alpha+(\\sin^{2} \\alpha+\\cos^{2} \\alpha)+2\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot \\sin \\alpha+1+2\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere we used $\\cos^{2} \\alpha+\\sin^{2} \\alpha=1$. Therefore, the simplified expression is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\cdot \\sin \\alpha+1+2\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha},\n$$ on the original domain $\\cos \\alpha\\ne0, \\sin \\alpha\\ne0$."] [:db/cas 17592186643108 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=15,$ $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!=96,$ and $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{96}\\,{\\theta }^{15}\\,{e}^{-10\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=15,\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!=96,\n$$ and $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{96}\\,{\\theta }^{15}\\,{e}^{-10\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186643117 :question/problem "Find the minor of  ${v}_{55}$ in  $V=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 8 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -7 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 5 & 7 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -6 & \\sqrt{5} & -21\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the minor of  ${v}_{55}$ in  $$\n\\displaystyle V=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 8 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -7 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 5 & 7 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -6 & \\sqrt{5} & -21\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186643133 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=7$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N=10$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{10-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,…,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{10-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{10-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{10-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{10-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (10-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,10n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{10n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{{x}_{i}})$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=20$ and $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{20}(1-\\theta )^{10(7)-20} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{20}(1-\\theta )^{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=7$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N=10$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{10-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,…,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{10-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{10-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{10-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{10-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (10-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,10n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{10n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{{x}_{i}})\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=20\n$$ and $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{20}(1-\\theta )^{10(7)-20} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{20}(1-\\theta )^{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186643139 :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186643145 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1+1+0+1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{3}(1-\\theta )^{4-3} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{3}(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1+1+0+1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{3}(1-\\theta )^{4-3} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{3}(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186643175 :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda$ of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda$ of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186643294 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix and $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $A$ and $1$ row in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication: \n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,1=(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,1=(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,1=(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,1=(-1)\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$AB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix and $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $A$ and $1$ row in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication: \n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,1=(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,1=(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,1=(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,1=(-1)\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle AB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186643454 :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{51}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{20}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{51}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{20}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186643456 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{88}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{56}=1?$" "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{88}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{56}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186643478 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1?$" "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186643484 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{33}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{61}=1?$" "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{33}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{61}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186644078 :question/problem "Simplify the expression ${[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)]}^{15}.$" "Simplify the expression $$\n\\displaystyle {[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)]}^{15}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186644104 :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{7}}.$" "Simplify the expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{7}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186644124 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=25$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $12$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =12\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(25-12)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +13\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=25$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $12$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =12\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(25-12)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +13\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186644166 :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{8}}.$" "Simplify the expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{8}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186645715 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A=\\{{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-5{x}^{2})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(2{x}^{3})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\}$" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\{{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-5{x}^{2})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(2{x}^{3})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle C=\\{{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186645741 :question/problem "Describe the set $S=\\{…,-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{1}{8},-\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{1}{32},...\\}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S.$*\n\n$$\nS=\\{{{field-1}}\\,:\\,n\\in {{field-2}}\\}\n$$" "Describe the set $$\n\\displaystyle S=\\{…,-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{1}{8},-\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{1}{32},...\\}\n$$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S.$*\n\n$$\nS=\\{{{field-1}}\\,:\\,n\\in {{field-2}}\\}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186645745 :question/problem "Let $f(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Find ${g}^{′}(1)$ given the function is defined by $g(x)={\\int }_{x}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.$" "Let $f(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Find ${g}^{′}(1)$ given the function is defined by $$\n\\displaystyle g(x)={\\int }_{x}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186645747 :question/problem "Write the following series in sigma notation in terms of index $n.$\n\n$32+16+8+…={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }${{field-1}}" "Write the following series in sigma notation in terms of index $n.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 32+16+8+…={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\n$${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186645753 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3x|>7 \\\\ 3|x|>7 \\\\ |x|>\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx<-\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=(-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{3})\\cup (\\frac{7}{3},\\infty ).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|3n|>7$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\{…,-4,-3\\}\\cup \\{3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|3q|>7$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,B,$ and $C.$\"" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3x|>7 \\\\ 3|x|>7 \\\\ |x|>\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx<-\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in I=(-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{3})\\cup (\\frac{7}{3},\\infty ).\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|3n|>7$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\{…,-4,-3\\}\\cup \\{3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|3q|>7$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,B,$ and $C.$\""] [:db/cas 17592186645762 :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{8}{5}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ and $x=4$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-coordinate is changing at the rate of $-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{8}{5}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}\n$$ and $x=4$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-coordinate is changing at the rate of $-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186646271 :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{3}{8}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{4}+C \\\\ \\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}(4-{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}(2-t)(2+t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=-2,0,2.$\n\nSince $t>0$, we discard solutions $t=-2,0.$\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{3}{8}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{4}+C \\\\ \\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $$\n\\displaystyle v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}(4-{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}(2-t)(2+t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=-2,0,2.$\n\nSince $t>0$, we discard solutions $t=-2,0.$\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186646280 :question/problem "Find the sum $\\frac{1-e}{e}-\\frac{(1-e)^{2}}{2{e}^{2}}+\\frac{(1-e)^{3}}{3{e}^{3}}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(1-e)^{n}}{n{e}^{n}}+⋯.$" "Find the sum $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1-e}{e}-\\frac{(1-e)^{2}}{2{e}^{2}}+\\frac{(1-e)^{3}}{3{e}^{3}}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(1-e)^{n}}{n{e}^{n}}+⋯.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186646294 :question/problem "Find ${M}^{T}$ given that \n    $M=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find ${M}^{T}$ given that \n    $$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186646300 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $(r-⟨1,-4,-4⟩)\\times ⟨6,3,-3⟩=0\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{x+2}{2}=\\frac{y-2}{1}=\\frac{z-2}{-1}.$" "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $(r-⟨1,-4,-4⟩)\\times ⟨6,3,-3⟩=0\\,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\frac{x+2}{2}=\\frac{y-2}{1}=\\frac{z-2}{-1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186646306 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are upper triangular matrices?\n\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Which of the following matrices are upper triangular matrices?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186646320 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $(r-⟨3,-4,-5⟩)\\times ⟨6,4,-4⟩=0\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{x+5}{-3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-5}{2}.$" "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $(r-⟨3,-4,-5⟩)\\times ⟨6,4,-4⟩=0\\,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\frac{x+5}{-3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-5}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186647013 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186647016 :question/problem "If\n$M=\\begin{bmatrix}a & -b & c\\end{bmatrix}$,\nthen find the transpose of $M.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${M}^{T}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}a & -b & c\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nthen find the transpose of $M.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${M}^{T}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186647052 :question/problem "Find $\\text{Im}(k)$ given that \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\overset{―}{kv}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 30\\text{i}-18\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find $\\text{Im}(k)$ given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\overset{―}{kv}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 30\\text{i}-18\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186647066 :question/problem "Find \n$\\begin{vmatrix}a & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & e & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}$\ngiven that \n$\\begin{vmatrix}a & -3 \\\\ 0 & e\\end{vmatrix}=6.$" "Find \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & e & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\ngiven that \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a & -3 \\\\ 0 & e\\end{vmatrix}=6.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186647093 :question/problem "Find ${R}^{T}$ given that \n    $R=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find ${R}^{T}$ given that \n    $$\n\\displaystyle R=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186647103 :question/problem "If\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},$\nfind $AB.$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$AB=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind $AB.$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$AB=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186647103 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix and $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $A$ and $1$ row in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication: \n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,12=-6\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -18 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-18=-6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -18 \\\\ -2 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-2=1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -18 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,3=1\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$AB=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -18 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix and $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $A$ and $1$ row in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication: \n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,12=-6\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -18 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-18=-6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -18 \\\\ -2 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-2=1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -18 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,3=1\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle AB=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -18 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186647783 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{6+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{8}\\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{6+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{8}\\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186647806 :question/problem "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$L=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -13 & 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle L=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -13 & 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186647833 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{7} & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2\\pi  & 3.1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & -3\\end{vmatrix}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{7} & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2\\pi  & 3.1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & -3\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186649158 :question/problem "$\\frac{5}{2}{y}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+3{y}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}y=${{field-1}} \n${y}^{4}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$y$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{2}{y}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+3{y}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}y=\n$${{field-1}} \n${y}^{4}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$y$"] [:db/cas 17592186649161 :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1,$ $z(0)=-1,$ and $w(0)=0,$ we place $y=1,$ $z=-1,$ and $w=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y+2z \\\\  & =0+1+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2-y+w \\\\  & =2-1+0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =yz-x \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)-0 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ |  | $-1$ | $1$ |  | $0$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{3}$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1+\\frac{1}{3}=-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=0+\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{3}$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{1}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈\\frac{2}{3},$\n$z\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈-\\frac{2}{3},$\nand\n$w\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈-\\frac{1}{3}.$" "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1,$ $z(0)=-1,$ and $w(0)=0,$ we place $y=1,$ $z=-1,$ and $w=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y+2z \\\\  & =0+1+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2-y+w \\\\  & =2-1+0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =yz-x \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)-0 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ |  | $-1$ | $1$ |  | $0$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{3}$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1+\\frac{1}{3}=-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=0+\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{3}$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{1}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$$\n\\displaystyle y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈\\frac{2}{3},\n$$\n$$\n\\displaystyle z\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈-\\frac{2}{3},\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle w\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186649187 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=f(x,y,z),\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=g(x,y,z),\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy &={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz &={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3}{2}-0=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+z \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+z)^{2}}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{(0+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $1$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{5}{2}$\nand\n$z\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{5}{2}.$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=f(x,y,z),\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=g(x,y,z),\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy &={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz &={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we will u