d discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=20$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $8$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(20-8)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=20$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $8$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(20-8)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186652275 :question/problem "$-3{a}^{2}(5{a}^{3}-\\frac{a}{8}+\\frac{1}{3})=${{field-1}}${a}^{5}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" "$$\n\\displaystyle -3{a}^{2}(5{a}^{3}-\\frac{a}{8}+\\frac{1}{3})=\n$${{field-1}}${a}^{5}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186652278 :question/problem "$-18x(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{xy}{3})=${{field-1}}\n$\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}y$" "$$\n\\displaystyle -18x(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{xy}{3})=\n$${{field-1}}\n$\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}y$"] [:db/cas 17592186652292 :question/problem "Given that $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{x-1}\\cdot ((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)+1)$" "Given that $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{x-1}\\cdot ((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\n$$\n{{field-1}}$)+1)$"] [:db/cas 17592186652323 :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y{y}^{″}+2({y}^{′})^{2} & =9x \\\\ 3y{y}^{″} & =9x-2({y}^{′})^{2} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{9x-2({y}^{′})^{2}}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{9x-2{z}^{2}}{3y}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(0)={y}^{′}(0)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(0)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{9x-2{z}^{2}}{3y},\\, && z(0)=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1}{2}-0=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{9x-2{z}^{2}}{3y} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 0-2\\cdot {1}^{2}}{3\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)}{(-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=z\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{4}{3}.$" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y{y}^{″}+2({y}^{′})^{2} & =9x \\\\ 3y{y}^{″} & =9x-2({y}^{′})^{2} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{9x-2({y}^{′})^{2}}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{9x-2{z}^{2}}{3y}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(0)={y}^{′}(0)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(0)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{9x-2{z}^{2}}{3y},\\, && z(0)=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1}{2}-0=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{9x-2{z}^{2}}{3y} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 0-2\\cdot {1}^{2}}{3\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)}{(-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle {y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=z\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186652331 :question/problem "$-4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\frac{x}{10}+\\frac{y}{20})=${{field-1}}\n$\\,-$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}{y}^{3}$" "$$\n\\displaystyle -4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\frac{x}{10}+\\frac{y}{20})=\n$${{field-1}}\n$\\,-$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}{y}^{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186652895 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{2x}{(2x-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2x}{(2x-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186653521 :question/problem "Simplify the product of permutations ${\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 4\\end{pmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1 & 3\\end{pmatrix}.$" "Simplify the product of permutations $$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 4\\end{pmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1 & 3\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186653532 :question/problem "Simplify the product of permutations ${\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 4 & 2 & 1\\end{pmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 5 & 4 & 1\\end{pmatrix}.$" "Simplify the product of permutations $$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 4 & 2 & 1\\end{pmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 5 & 4 & 1\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186653534 :question/problem "Simplify the product of permutations ${\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & 3\\end{pmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 & 4\\end{pmatrix}.$" "Simplify the product of permutations $$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & 3\\end{pmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 & 4\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186653543 :question/problem "Simplify the product of permutations ${\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 4 & 5\\end{pmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5 & 1 & 2\\end{pmatrix}.$" "Simplify the product of permutations $$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 4 & 5\\end{pmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5 & 1 & 2\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186653553 :question/problem "Simplify the product of permutations ${\\left( \\begin{array}{cccc}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4 & 2\\end{array} \\right)}^{-1} {\\left( \\begin{array}{cccc}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 4\\end{array} \\right)}.$" "Simplify the product of permutations $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left( \\begin{array}{cccc}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4 & 2\\end{array} \\right)}^{-1} {\\left( \\begin{array}{cccc}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 4\\end{array} \\right)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186653586 :question/problem "Consider the two sets of $6$ paired observations shown in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $2$ | $3$ | $9$ | $7$ | $5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $16$ | $15$ | $14$ | $23$ | $18$ | $15$ |\n\nGiven that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}^{2}=184,$ calculate ${S}_{xx}.$" "Consider the two sets of $6$ paired observations shown in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $2$ | $3$ | $9$ | $7$ | $5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $16$ | $15$ | $14$ | $23$ | $18$ | $15$ |\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}^{2}=184,\n$$ calculate ${S}_{xx}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186653586 :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${x}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ data:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}=2+3+9+7+5+4=30.\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}^{2}=184.$\n\nFinally, by substituting into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}=184-\\frac{1}{6}(30)^{2}=184-150=34.\n$$" "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${x}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ data:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}=2+3+9+7+5+4=30.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}^{2}=184.\n$$\n\nFinally, by substituting into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}=184-\\frac{1}{6}(30)^{2}=184-150=34.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186653587 :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,3-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (3-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+{C}_{1},\\,3t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=⟨\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}⟩.$ Substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{1},\\,3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}⟩ & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{4}+{C}_{1},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=\\pi$ and ${C}_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+\\pi ,\\,3t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=\\pi$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )+\\pi ,\\,3\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi ,3\\pi +2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,3-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (3-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+{C}_{1},\\,3t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=⟨\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}⟩.\n$$ Substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{1},\\,3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}⟩ & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{4}+{C}_{1},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=\\pi$ and ${C}_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+\\pi ,\\,3t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=\\pi$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )+\\pi ,\\,3\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi ,3\\pi +2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186653588 :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7185759e7ed2211fa82e39b3731969a2593645a4da90ce46289b290ff4c32fd.png)\n\nConsider the semicircle and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$" "![](images/d7/d7185759e7ed2211fa82e39b3731969a2593645a4da90ce46289b290ff4c32fd.png)\n\nConsider the semicircle and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186653588 :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works against the particle for $y<0,$ while $F$ works with the particle for $y>0.$ \n\n![](images/30/306fdb7f7a6054d214940d1ca9614c35905f015f34d175561a7850781b751a93.png)\n\n \nBy symmetry, the contribution to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $y<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $y>0.$ So, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/c9/c9ea0397e7ba2a2ce6aef1058c6935a3282e12df5adc3b3d564cfb87c5f76c3b.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works against the particle for $y<0,$ while $F$ works with the particle for $y>0.$ \n\n![](images/30/306fdb7f7a6054d214940d1ca9614c35905f015f34d175561a7850781b751a93.png)\n\n \nBy symmetry, the contribution to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $y<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $y>0.$ So, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/c9/c9ea0397e7ba2a2ce6aef1058c6935a3282e12df5adc3b3d564cfb87c5f76c3b.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186653595 :question/problem "Simplify the product of permutations ${\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{pmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 3\\end{pmatrix}.$" "Simplify the product of permutations $$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{pmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 3\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186654246 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$M=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & 9 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{7}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & 9 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{7}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186654275 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the line $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T},$ we first use the linearity of the transformation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(r) & =T(p+tv) \\\\  & =T(p)+T(tv) \\\\  & =T(p)+tT(v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the images of $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(p) & =T\\,p \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(v) & =T\\,v \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the image is $\\mathbf{r} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]} + t {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ - 4 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.$" "To find the image of the line $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T},$ we first use the linearity of the transformation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(r) & =T(p+tv) \\\\  & =T(p)+T(tv) \\\\  & =T(p)+tT(v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the images of $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(p) & =T\\,p \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(v) & =T\\,v \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the image is $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{r} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]} + t {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ - 4 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186654292 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$M=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 11 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 11 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186654297 :question/worked-solution "The equation of the line using the cross product takes the general form\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p}) \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}$ is the direction vector of the line and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point that lies in the line.\n\nFrom the equation of our line with cross product, we obtain that its vector equation is \n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} + t {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo find the image of the line $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T},$ we first use the linearity of the transformation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(r) & =T(p+tv) \\\\  & =T(p)+T(tv) \\\\  & =T(p)+tT(v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the images of $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(p) & =T\\,p \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(v) & =T\\,v \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the image is $\\mathbf{r} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]} + t {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}.$" "The equation of the line using the cross product takes the general form\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p}) \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}$ is the direction vector of the line and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point that lies in the line.\n\nFrom the equation of our line with cross product, we obtain that its vector equation is \n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} + t {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo find the image of the line $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T},$ we first use the linearity of the transformation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(r) & =T(p+tv) \\\\  & =T(p)+T(tv) \\\\  & =T(p)+tT(v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the images of $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(p) & =T\\,p \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(v) & =T\\,v \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the image is $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{r} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]} + t {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186654324 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nFrom the Cartesian equation of our line, we obtain that its vector equation is \n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]} + t {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo find the image of the line $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T},$ we first use the linearity of the transformation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(r) & =T(p+tv) \\\\  & =T(p)+T(tv) \\\\  & =T(p)+tT(v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the images of $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(p) & =T\\,p \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(v) & =T\\,v \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the image is $\\mathbf{r} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]} + t {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}6 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 11\\end{array} \\right]}.$" "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nFrom the Cartesian equation of our line, we obtain that its vector equation is \n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]} + t {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo find the image of the line $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T},$ we first use the linearity of the transformation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(r) & =T(p+tv) \\\\  & =T(p)+T(tv) \\\\  & =T(p)+tT(v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the images of $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(p) & =T\\,p \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(v) & =T\\,v \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the image is $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{r} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]} + t {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}6 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 11\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186654772 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $2$st one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$  which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $2$st one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For $\\lambda =3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$  which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186654811 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}=1?$" "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186655474 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$K=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle K=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186655487 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the total area enclosed by graph of the polar curve $r=\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )}{2{\\theta }^{2}}$ for $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}?$" "Which of the following gives the total area enclosed by graph of the polar curve $r=\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )}{2{\\theta }^{2}}$ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186655490 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N=9$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{9-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,…,9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{9-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{9-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{9-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{9-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (9-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,9n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{9n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{{x}_{i}})$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =5+8+3+7 \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{23}(1-\\theta )^{9(4)-23} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{23}(1-\\theta )^{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N=9$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{9-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,…,9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{9-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{9-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{9-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{9-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (9-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,9n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{9n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{{x}_{i}})\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =5+8+3+7 \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{23}(1-\\theta )^{9(4)-23} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{23}(1-\\theta )^{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186655494 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4x}{3-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4x}{3-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186655497 :question/problem "![](images/8e/8ee2e0735c8a0dca21e605cef10fd288c5baff39f3a7acf82ad31a67b6c96621.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown above." "![](images/8e/8ee2e0735c8a0dca21e605cef10fd288c5baff39f3a7acf82ad31a67b6c96621.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$ as shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186655499 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=7$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1+1+1+2+3+2+2 \\\\  & =12 \\\\ C & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})! \\\\  & =1!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 3!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 2! \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 6\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{48}\\,{\\theta }^{12}\\,{e}^{-7\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=7$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1+1+1+2+3+2+2 \\\\  & =12 \\\\ C & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})! \\\\  & =1!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 3!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 2! \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 6\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{48}\\,{\\theta }^{12}\\,{e}^{-7\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186655515 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0+2+3+2+1+0 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ C & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})! \\\\  & =0!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 3!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 0! \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot 6\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{24}\\,{\\theta }^{8}\\,{e}^{-6\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0+2+3+2+1+0 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ C & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})! \\\\  & =0!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 3!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 0! \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot 6\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{24}\\,{\\theta }^{8}\\,{e}^{-6\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186655529 :question/problem "The weights, in kilograms, of six randomly selected watermelons drawn from a population were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n1.45,\\,1.54,\\,1.30,\\,1.65,\\,1.35,\\,1.23\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}$ for this particular sample." "The weights, in kilograms, of six randomly selected watermelons drawn from a population were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n1.45,\\,1.54,\\,1.30,\\,1.65,\\,1.35,\\,1.23\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$ for this particular sample."] [:db/cas 17592186655530 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N=4$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{4-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,3,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (4-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,4n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{4n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =2+1+4+2+3 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{12}(1-\\theta )^{4(5)-12} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{12}(1-\\theta )^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N=4$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{4-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,3,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (4-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,4n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{4n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =2+1+4+2+3 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{12}(1-\\theta )^{4(5)-12} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{12}(1-\\theta )^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186655544 :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ If $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}|{X}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2}|$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}({X}_{i}-\\sigma )^{2}$\n\nIII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}\\frac{{X}_{i}+\\sigma }{{\\sigma }^{2}+1}$" "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ If $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}|{X}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2}|\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}({X}_{i}-\\sigma )^{2}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}\\frac{{X}_{i}+\\sigma }{{\\sigma }^{2}+1}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186655545 :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (5{t}^{2}-3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =0+0+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=\\frac{5}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+2.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{5}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3}+2t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(0)=0.$ Substituting this