nce relations are linear (with constant coefficients)?\n\nI. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2}+\\frac{{a}_{n-2}}{3}$\n\nII. ${b}_{n}={b}_{n-1}-\\frac{n}{3}$\n\nIII. ${c}_{n}={c}_{n-1}+2{c}_{n-2}-\\frac{{c}_{n-3}}{3}$" "Which of the following recurrence relations are linear (with constant coefficients)?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {a}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2}+\\frac{{a}_{n-2}}{3}\n$$\n\nII. ${b}_{n}={b}_{n-1}-\\frac{n}{3}$\n\nIII. ${c}_{n}={c}_{n-1}+2{c}_{n-2}-\\frac{{c}_{n-3}}{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186657446 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi /30}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /10}}10xcsc\\,(5{x}^{2})cot\\,(5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi /30}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /10}}10xcsc\\,(5{x}^{2})cot\\,(5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186657452 :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨-{csc}^{2}⁡(t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (-{csc}^{2}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=⟨1,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩.$ Substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)=0 & \\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)=0 & \\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So, this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨-{csc}^{2}⁡(t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (-{csc}^{2}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=⟨1,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩.\n$$ Substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)=0 & \\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)=0 & \\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So, this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."] [:db/cas 17592186658279 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{x}?$\n\nI. Its range is $(\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).$\n\nII. Its horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nIII. It passes through the point $(1,1).$" "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{x}?\n$$\n\nI. Its range is $(\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).$\n\nII. Its horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nIII. It passes through the point $(1,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186658279 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/30/30d22c0b94142f12be18470b4de1628ae03622326b9b7f24777eefdb982eecce.png)\n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. The horizontal asymptote of $y={\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{x}$ is $y=0$, and it remains the same after the vertical scaling.\n\n- Statement III is true. $f(1)=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}=1.$ Therefore, the point $(1,1)$ lies on the curve.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." "Let's start by recalling the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{x}:\n$$\n\n![](images/30/30d22c0b94142f12be18470b4de1628ae03622326b9b7f24777eefdb982eecce.png)\n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. The horizontal asymptote of $y={\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{x}$ is $y=0$, and it remains the same after the vertical scaling.\n\n- Statement III is true. $$\n\\displaystyle f(1)=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}=1.\n$$ Therefore, the point $(1,1)$ lies on the curve.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."] [:db/cas 17592186658297 :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}=-2,\\,{x}_{2}=5,\\,{x}_{3}=-7,\\,{x}_{4}=0,\\,{x}_{5}=3\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}+2)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot (-1)=$ {{field-1}}." "$$\n{x}_{1}=-2,\\,{x}_{2}=5,\\,{x}_{3}=-7,\\,{x}_{4}=0,\\,{x}_{5}=3\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}+2)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot (-1)=\n$$ {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186658911 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186658924 :question/worked-solution "The Spearman's rank correlation coefficient ${r}_{s}$ is Pearson's correlation coefficient applied to two sets of ranks. \n\nGiven two sets of ranks ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ where the numbers in each set are uniquely expressed as integers between $1$ and $n,$ the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n{r}_{s}=1-\\frac{6}{n({n}^{2}-1)}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{d}_{i}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${d}_{i}={x}_{i}-{y}_{i}$ is the difference between the ranks of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the differences between the ranks, and their squares.\n\n| ${x}_{i}$ | $4$ | $5$ | $3$ | $1$ | $2$ | $6$ | ${\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}=21$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${y}_{i}$ | $2$ | $3$ | $6$ | $4$ | $5$ | $1$ | ${\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}=21$ |\n| ${d}_{i}$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $5$ | ${\\sum }_{i}{d}_{i}=0$ |\n| ${d}_{i}^{2}$ | $4$ | $4$ | $9$ | $9$ | $9$ | $25$ | ${\\sum }_{i}{d}_{i}^{2}=60$ |\n\nNotice that ${\\sum }_{i}{d}_{i}=0$ as expected.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{d}_{i}^{2}=60,\\,n=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient is\n\n$$\n{r}_{s}=1-\\frac{6(60)}{6({6}^{2}-1)}≈-0.71.\n$$" "The Spearman's rank correlation coefficient ${r}_{s}$ is Pearson's correlation coefficient applied to two sets of ranks. \n\nGiven two sets of ranks ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ where the numbers in each set are uniquely expressed as integers between $1$ and $n,$ the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n{r}_{s}=1-\\frac{6}{n({n}^{2}-1)}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{d}_{i}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${d}_{i}={x}_{i}-{y}_{i}$ is the difference between the ranks of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the differences between the ranks, and their squares.\n\n| ${x}_{i}$ | $4$ | $5$ | $3$ | $1$ | $2$ | $6$ | ${\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}=21$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${y}_{i}$ | $2$ | $3$ | $6$ | $4$ | $5$ | $1$ | ${\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}=21$ |\n| ${d}_{i}$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $5$ | ${\\sum }_{i}{d}_{i}=0$ |\n| ${d}_{i}^{2}$ | $4$ | $4$ | $9$ | $9$ | $9$ | $25$ | $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i}{d}_{i}^{2}=60\n$$ |\n\nNotice that ${\\sum }_{i}{d}_{i}=0$ as expected.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{d}_{i}^{2}=60,\\,n=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient is\n\n$$\n{r}_{s}=1-\\frac{6(60)}{6({6}^{2}-1)}≈-0.71.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186658930 :question/worked-solution "The Spearman's rank correlation coefficient ${r}_{s}$ is Pearson's correlation coefficient applied to two sets of ranks. \n\nGiven two sets of ranks ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ where the numbers in each set are uniquely expressed as integers between $1$ and $n,$ the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n{r}_{s}=1-\\frac{6}{n({n}^{2}-1)}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{d}_{i}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${d}_{i}={x}_{i}-{y}_{i}$ is the difference between the ranks of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}.$\n\nIn our case, we are given that $n=9$ and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{d}_{i}^{2}=161.$ \n\nTherefore, Spearman's correlation coefficient is\n\n$$\n{r}_{s}=1-\\frac{6(161)}{9({9}^{2}-1)}≈-0.34.\n$$\n\nSince $-0.7<{r}_{s}\\le -0.3,$ we conclude that there is $\\text{a weak negative correlation}$ between the rankings." "The Spearman's rank correlation coefficient ${r}_{s}$ is Pearson's correlation coefficient applied to two sets of ranks. \n\nGiven two sets of ranks ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ where the numbers in each set are uniquely expressed as integers between $1$ and $n,$ the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n{r}_{s}=1-\\frac{6}{n({n}^{2}-1)}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{d}_{i}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${d}_{i}={x}_{i}-{y}_{i}$ is the difference between the ranks of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}.$\n\nIn our case, we are given that $n=9$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{d}_{i}^{2}=161.\n$$ \n\nTherefore, Spearman's correlation coefficient is\n\n$$\n{r}_{s}=1-\\frac{6(161)}{9({9}^{2}-1)}≈-0.34.\n$$\n\nSince $-0.7<{r}_{s}\\le -0.3,$ we conclude that there is $\\text{a weak negative correlation}$ between the rankings."] [:db/cas 17592186658945 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡(x)+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡(x)+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186658964 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int 2{x}^{2}{csc}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2{x}^{2}{csc}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186658965 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186658974 :question/worked-solution "The Spearman's rank correlation coefficient ${r}_{s}$ is Pearson's correlation coefficient applied to two sets of ranks. \n\nGiven two sets of ranks ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ where the numbers in each set are uniquely expressed as integers between $1$ and $n,$ the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n{r}_{s}=1-\\frac{6}{n({n}^{2}-1)}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{d}_{i}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${d}_{i}={x}_{i}-{y}_{i}$ is the difference between the ranks of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the differences between the ranks, and their squares.\n\n| ${x}_{i}$ | $1$ | $4$ | $2$ | $3$ | ${\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}=10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${y}_{i}$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ | ${\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}=10$ |\n| ${d}_{i}$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $2$ | ${\\sum }_{i}{d}_{i}=0$ |\n| ${d}_{i}^{2}$ | $4$ | $4$ | $4$ | $4$ | ${\\sum }_{i}{d}_{i}^{2}=16$ |\n\nNotice that ${\\sum }_{i}{d}_{i}=0$ as expected.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{d}_{i}^{2}=16,\\,n=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient is\n\n$$\n{r}_{s}=1-\\frac{6(16)}{4({4}^{2}-1)}=-0.6.\n$$" "The Spearman's rank correlation coefficient ${r}_{s}$ is Pearson's correlation coefficient applied to two sets of ranks. \n\nGiven two sets of ranks ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ where the numbers in each set are uniquely expressed as integers between $1$ and $n,$ the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n{r}_{s}=1-\\frac{6}{n({n}^{2}-1)}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{d}_{i}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${d}_{i}={x}_{i}-{y}_{i}$ is the difference between the ranks of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the differences between the ranks, and their squares.\n\n| ${x}_{i}$ | $1$ | $4$ | $2$ | $3$ | ${\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}=10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${y}_{i}$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ | ${\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}=10$ |\n| ${d}_{i}$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $2$ | ${\\sum }_{i}{d}_{i}=0$ |\n| ${d}_{i}^{2}$ | $4$ | $4$ | $4$ | $4$ | $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i}{d}_{i}^{2}=16\n$$ |\n\nNotice that ${\\sum }_{i}{d}_{i}=0$ as expected.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{d}_{i}^{2}=16,\\,n=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient is\n\n$$\n{r}_{s}=1-\\frac{6(16)}{4({4}^{2}-1)}=-0.6.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186658981 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int x\\operatorname{csc}⁡({x}^{2})\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{csc}⁡({x}^{2})\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186659673 :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)}^{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186659673 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=2{\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{8}{9}.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{2{\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)}^{2}}{2{\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)}^{1}}=\\frac{4}{9}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)}{(1-\\frac{4}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{5}{9}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum a{r}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=2{\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{8}{9}.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{2{\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)}^{2}}{2{\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)}^{1}}=\\frac{4}{9}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)}{(1-\\frac{4}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{5}{9}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186659677 :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} & -2\\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the product $AB$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} & -2\\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186659684 :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186659689 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186659708 :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n}.$" "Evaluate the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186659732 :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186659744 :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186659752 :question/problem "Find $n$ if $\\frac{{n}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "Find $n$ if $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{n}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186660329 :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-4I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-4I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $2a+b=2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+2=-1.$" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-4I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-4I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $2a+b=2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+2=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186660336 :question/problem "![](images/83/831e69186176b13168f2272d55d0535694b0b78c98d7b26a6ff00dbaa1b0b93b.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the line $x=0$ and the curves $y=4-{x}^{2}$ and $y=x-2,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" "![](images/83/831e69186176b13168f2272d55d0535694b0b78c98d7b26a6ff00dbaa1b0b93b.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the line $x=0$ and the curves $y=4-{x}^{2}$ and $y=x-2,$ as shown above, then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186660353 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody>\n<tr height=\"35px\">\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"4\">\n${B}^{-1}=\\,\\,$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">\n$1$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"35px\">\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        {{field-1}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"35px\">\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n         {{field-2}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        {{field-3}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"35px\">\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        {{field-4}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" "Find the inverse of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody>\n<tr height=\"35px\">\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"4\">\n${B}^{-1}=\\,\\,$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">\n$1$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"35px\">\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        {{field-1}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"35px\">\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n         {{field-2}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        {{field-3}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr heigh