peratorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}≈9.34$ and $n=8.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 9.34 \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -9.34(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b≈8+9.34=17.34.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=8$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}≈9.34\n$$ and $n=8.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 9.34 \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -9.34(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b≈8+9.34=17.34.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186662816 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=20$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}≈23.25$ and $n=20.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈20\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 23.25 \\\\  & =20\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -23.25(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b≈20+23.25=43.25.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=20$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}≈23.25\n$$ and $n=20.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈20\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 23.25 \\\\  & =20\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -23.25(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b≈20+23.25=43.25.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186662834 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\prod }_{j=1}^{4}(4j-1).$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{j=1}^{4}(4j-1).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186662835 :question/problem "${\\prod }_{k=2}^{5}\\frac{k-1}{k+1}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{k=2}^{5}\\frac{k-1}{k+1}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186662837 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=8$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}=31.4,$ ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2}=132.52,$ and $n=8.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{8}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(132.52-2\\theta \\cdot 31.4+8{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-66.26)+31.4\\theta +(-4){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-66.26)+31.4+(-4)=-38.86.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=8$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}=31.4,\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2}=132.52,\n$$ and $n=8.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{8}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(132.52-2\\theta \\cdot 31.4+8{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-66.26)+31.4\\theta +(-4){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-66.26)+31.4+(-4)=-38.86.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186662841 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=12$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}=32.3$ and $n=12.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =12\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 32.3 \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-32.3)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=12+(-32.3)=-20.3.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=12$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}=32.3\n$$ and $n=12.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =12\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 32.3 \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-32.3)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=12+(-32.3)=-20.3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186662842 :question/problem "${\\prod }_{m=4}^{p}(3m-2)=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{m=4}^{p}(3m-2)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186662846 :question/problem "${\\prod }_{n=6}^{m}(4n+1)=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{n=6}^{m}(4n+1)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186662851 :question/problem "${\\prod }_{j=2}^{6}\\frac{2j}{j+1}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{j=2}^{6}\\frac{2j}{j+1}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186662857 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\prod }_{j=3}^{6}\\frac{3j+1}{2j}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\prod }_{j=3}^{6}\\frac{3j+1}{2j}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186663379 :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{2}=1$ and the plane $x-2y=3$  onto the $yz$-plane." "Find the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{2}=1\n$$ and the plane $x-2y=3$  onto the $yz$-plane."] [:db/cas 17592186663379 :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx-2y=3\\,⟹\\,x=3+2y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the hyperboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(3+2y-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the hyperbola $\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{2}=1.$" "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx-2y=3\\,⟹\\,x=3+2y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the hyperboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(3+2y-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{9{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{2}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186663394 :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9}=1$ and the plane $x-y=2$ onto the $xz$-plane." "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9}=1\n$$ and the plane $x-y=2$ onto the $xz$-plane."] [:db/cas 17592186663394 :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $y.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y &=2\\,⟹\\,y=x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $y$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x+4}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2{x}^{2}-4x+4}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+2}{3}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+2}{3}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{({x}^{2}-2x+1)+1}{3}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}+1}{3}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{6} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{6}=1.$" "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $y.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y &=2\\,⟹\\,y=x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $y$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x+4}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2{x}^{2}-4x+4}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+2}{3}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+2}{3}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{({x}^{2}-2x+1)+1}{3}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}+1}{3}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{6} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{6}=1.\n$$"]]
